\begin_layout Subsubsection
From numerical data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once MODFLOW has finished,
 the computed heads and cell-by-cell flows can be analyzed and visualized.
 The most inportant information to be obtained from the computed heads is the so-called GLG,
 GHG and GVG (combined in the acronym GXG).
 GLG is the mean lowest water table,
 the GHG the mean heighest water table and the GVG the mean spring water table.
 These 
\emph on
means
\emph default
 are taken over the 8 most recent consecutive years,
 while the hightest watertable in any year is by agreement the mean of the three highest water tables in that year as measured bi-monthly on the 14th and 28th of each month.
 Likewise for the lowest watertable.
 The mean spring water table is defined as the mean water table of 14 March,
 1 April and 14 April in the same year (check,
 because different definitions may be applied here).
 These GXG can be shown on a map.
 Trends in GXG may reflect climate variations of change,
 which may also be presented with the GXG averaged over e set of parcels can be shown as a function of time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A useful data structure for the head in all parcels will be 
\begin_inset Formula $head(t,layer,row).$
\end_inset

 Notice that each Modlfow row corresponds to a parcel.
 This structure is obtained from the MODFLOW-computed heads,
 which has the structure 
\begin_inset Formula $h(t,layer,row.col)$
\end_inset

,
 by averaging over the active columns (= parcel width),
 weighted by involved cell areas.
 Notice that each row is one m wide,
 which correspons to cross sections of unit width.
 This head structure can be extended by one column to indicate whether any particular time corresponds to the three highest or three lowest water levels in the year implied by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The data structure is then 
\begin_inset Formula $h(t,layer,row,hit)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $hit$
\end_inset

 is 0,
 1,
 2,
 3 depending on whether for this parcel (row) the time corresponds to one of the the three lowest (
\begin_inset Formula $hit=1$
\end_inset

),
 the three highest (
\begin_inset Formula $hit=2$
\end_inset

) or spring (
\begin_inset Formula $hit=3$
\end_inset

) water levels in the year implied by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 This head data object holds all the head information required for the visualization and allows easy extraction the GXGs.
 This yields 3 array:
 
\begin_inset Formula $GLG(row)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $GHG(row)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $GVG(row)$
\end_inset

 or just one array 
\begin_inset Formula $GXG(row,0:3)$
\end_inset

 with columns corresponding to 
\emph on
GLG
\emph default
,
 
\emph on
GHG
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
GVG
\emph default
 respectively.
\end_layout

